    {
      "id": "GO-TEXTID",
      "title": "question: horizon.Offer reads an offer id given as \"+16\" or \"-0\"",
      "component": "go-stellar-sdk",
      "severity": "divergence",
      "caps": [
        "CAP-0003",
        "CAP-0006"
      ],
      "minimal_entry": "cap03.offer-liabilities.synth-textual-offer-id-noncanonical",
      "reproduce": "shapekit conformance run --sdk go --version v0.9.0 --entry cap03.offer-liabilities.synth-textual-offer-id-noncanonical",
      "status": "draft",
      "draft": "candidate/findings/issues/go-cap03.offer-liabilities.synth-textual-offer-id-noncanonical.md",
      "cause": "horizon.Offer.ID is an int64 tagged `json:\"id,string\"`, so encoding/json unquotes the text and passes it to strconv.ParseInt, which takes an optional sign: \"+16\" reads as 16 and \"-0\" as 0, on every tested version (v0.7.1 to v0.9.0). The field reads horizon's own responses, which carry the canonical text, so the draft is a question. rs-stellar-xdr reads the same two strings the same way (RS-1, rs-stellar-xdr#627); the code is separate, so this is its own finding with RS-1 related.",
